# DESCRIPTION

regulator-fixed drives a devicetree's fixed regulator: a supply at one voltage, switched on and off by a GPIO, or always on.
devmgr(8) starts one for each node that matches its record, after the GPIO's provider and the regulator's own supply's, and before the devices that link to it:

```text
driver regulator-fixed
class power
match dt regulator-fixed
```

It reads its node's properties at `/dev/dt/node`: `regulator-name`, `regulator-min-microvolt`, `enable-active-high`, `regulator-always-on`, `regulator-boot-on` and `startup-delay-us`.
Its GPIO is on at its high level with `enable-active-high` and at its low level without it, whatever the specifier's flags say, as Linux reads the binding.
Turning it on turns on its own supply (`vin-supply`) first, sets its GPIO through the GPIO's provider (k1-gpio(3)'s `gpio <bank> <pin> 0 on|off`), then waits `startup-delay-us`.
A regulator that is always on or on at boot is turned on when the driver starts; one with no GPIO is on, and can't be turned off.
A device's `<name>-supply` names it, and its driver turns it on through this tree before the device, as the Orange Pi RV2's PCIe slots' `vpcie3v3-supply`, GPIO 116, is.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/regulator-fixed-<node>`, and at `/dev/provider/<service>` in the drivers of the devices that link to it.

: `ctl`
  Takes `on` or `off`, a line each.
  Another line, `off` with no GPIO, or a provider that refuses fails the write as invalid, and the driver logs which.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `<name> <microvolts> uV on|off`; then its GPIO, `gpio <provider> <cells> active-high|active-low`, where it has one; then `commands <n>, failed <n>`, and `last failure: on|off: <why>`.

